/**
 * The one operation a bf16 value still has no overload for is `%`, and not because of the
 * precision: `%` is refused on every floating type, f32 included (see
 * test/cases/compile/operators/float_mod_assign.cml), so `__mod__` is registered for the integer
 * types only and a call that meets a bf16 value there has no candidate. That is the case
 * SemanticDiag::ReducedPrecisionUnsupported is about (design principles 7.3): it is located at the
 * operator, names it and the element type, rather than listing overloads the call could never
 * match. The refusal is part of compiling the program, so the empty array is never read at run
 * time. The arithmetic and comparison a bf16 value does have -- `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `**`, `==`,
 * `<`... (the rule item (b)) -- are not refused, and test/cases/modules/stdlib/reduced_scalar.cml
 * pins what they compute. This case used to assert the refusal of `+`, which was true before that
 * item; it is re-pointed at the operation that is genuinely refused rather than deleted, so the
 * diagnostic keeps a case that exercises it.
 */
module stdlib_reduced_arithmetic_refused

func main(): int sync {
    let xs: bf16[] = []
    println(xs[0] % xs[0])
    return 0
}